\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Unless a section says otherwise, ${\mathbb{N}}=\{1,2,\ldots\}$,
${\mathbb{Z}}$ is the ring of integers, ${\mathbb{Q}},{\mathbb{R}},{\mathbb{C}}$ are the rational, real, and complex
fields, $[N]=\{1,\ldots,\lfloor N\rfloor\}$, and $\log$ is the natural
logarithm. For real functions of a parameter tending to infinity,
$f\ll g$ and $f=O(g)$ mean $|f|\leq Cg$ eventually for some fixed $C$;
$f\gg g$ reverses this comparison; $f=o(g)$ means $f/g\to0$;
$f\sim g$ means $f/g\to1$; and $f\asymp g$ means both order bounds.
A subscript on $O$ or $\ll$ specifies allowed constant dependence.
The expression $n^{o(1)}$ in an upper bound means growth smaller than
$n^\epsilon$ for each fixed $\epsilon>0$. Local definitions govern any
exception, finite-field convention, or use of the same symbol for another
object. An asymptotic claim is not automatically an effective algorithm.



The Hamming weight $w(x)$ counts nonzero coordinates and $H_n(r)=\{x\in{\mathbb{F}}_2^n:w(x)\leq r\}$. A subspace $V$ has covering radius at most $r$ when $V+H_n(r)={\mathbb{F}}_2^n$. Its covering density is $|V||H_n(r)|/2^n$. For fixed $r$, $f(r)$ is the least limiting density obtainable along unbounded lengths as in the statement; the subsequent question lets $r$ tend to infinity. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]} \href{https://www.mathnet.ru/eng/ppi628}{[S2]}

\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $r$ be fixed and let $H(r)$ be the Hamming ball of radius $r$ in $\mathbb{F}_2^n$. Let $f(r)$ be the smallest constant such that there exist infinitely many $n$ with subspaces $V_n \leq \mathbb{F}_2^n$ satisfying $V_n + H(r) = \mathbb{F}_2^n$ and $|V_n| = (f(r) + o(1)) \frac{2^n}{|H(r)|}$. Does $f(r) \to \infty$? \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\paragraph{Residual task reported by the archive.}

The embedded review (2026-08-17) records: Prove an unbounded lower bound for the asymptotic density of linear covering codes as the radius grows, or construct bounded-density families refuting divergence. \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
As the allowed error radius increases, must binary linear covering codes become increasingly inefficient relative to the elementary sphere-covering bound?

